From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Seabraid <seabraid@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 1 Jan 2000 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] seabraid: bound every helper-written string the engine
 reads

The helper is a separate process the engine does not trust. The engine
read its shared-memory text (msg, helper_name, err, tier_err, pf_err) with
%s, std::string(const char*) and "terminate the last byte, then read": a
helper that fills a field to its full width without a NUL made the engine
read past it, and the terminating store raced the helper's own writes.

Add seabraid::bounded_text(field, cap) to the channel header: it copies at
most cap-1 bytes, stops at the first NUL, never reads past the field, and
drops every byte outside printable ASCII except TAB so helper text cannot
inject terminal escape sequences into logs or error strings. Use it at
every engine-side read of a helper-written char array (remote_experts,
remote_prefill); the racy terminate-then-read stores go.

Channel::connect_private and the private attach now return the structured
Attach { kAttached, kNotReady, kRefused } instead of a bool the caller had
to interpret by matching the English error text.

seabraid_bounded_text_test (CPU only, registered in the root CMakeLists) fills each
field to its full width with no NUL, on exact-width heap buffers (ASan
aborts on any over-read) and on a real shm header, and checks NUL stop,
cap 0/1, null and control-byte stripping.

The include/strata/seabraid/channel.hpp (and expert_spec.hpp) half of this change lives in Seabraid's
adapters/strata/wire, the wire's one owner; this patch keeps only the engine side.
